(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-disorders-in-immunity-quiz"]={"title":"Disorders in Immunity — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is said about the course of atopic allergies over a lifetime?","choices":["Lifelong, outgrown, or appearing later","They are always lifelong once established","They only ever appear in childhood","They resolve in every patient by adulthood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and allergy can also be affected by age, infection and geographic area, so the picture is not fixed at any point. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why do allergic reactions use the same machinery as antiparasitic defense?","choices":["Allergens are all derived from parasites","The immune system cannot produce IgG against allergens","Many allergens resemble parasitic antigens","Parasites and allergens are both intracellular"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the same cells and mechanisms that defend against multicellular parasites are the ones involved in allergic reactions. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"When does an Rh negative person produce anti-Rh antibodies?","choices":["Only if they are also ABO incompatible","Only after sensitization","From birth, without any exposure","Whenever they encounter any foreign protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — unlike ABO, where antibodies against the antigens you lack are already present, Rh requires prior exposure. A bad transfusion or an incompatible pregnancy does it. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which four categories of immunopathology are described?","choices":["Infection, inflammation, necrosis and repair","Bacterial, viral, fungal and parasitic disease","Innate, adaptive, humoral and cellular","Allergy, autoimmunity, immunodeficiency and cancer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and they sort neatly by direction: allergy and autoimmunity are overreactions, while immunodeficiency and cancer are underreactions. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How is hemolytic disease of the newborn prevented?","choices":["Transfusing the mother with Rh positive blood","Cross-matching the fetus against the mother","Passive immunization with anti-Rh antibody","Active immunization of the mother against Rh"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — Rhogam prevents sensitization of the mother, which is why it must be given before her own response can develop. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which are the general targets of a type I reaction?","choices":["Liver and spleen","Joints and kidneys","Skin, airway, gut and conjunctiva","Bone marrow and lymph nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, producing rashes, itching, redness, rhinitis, sneezing, diarrhea and tears respectively. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why does it matter to distinguish allergy from infection?","choices":["Infection never produces respiratory symptoms","Allergy testing can also diagnose infection","They present similarly but differ entirely","Allergy is always more severe than infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and this is why diagnosis uses objective methods — skin testing, blood tests and supervised challenge — rather than symptoms alone. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which autoimmune disorders are named as type III?","choices":["Multiple sclerosis and type 1 diabetes","Graves disease and Hashimoto thyroiditis","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis and lupus","Myasthenia gravis and autoimmune hemolytic anemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and all three fit the pattern: immune complexes depositing in joints, skin and kidney, the tissues typical of complex disease. Rheumatoid arthritis too. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What causes serum sickness?","choices":["A second vaccine injection at the same site","Inhalation of a protein allergen","Contact with metals or cosmetics","Exposure to foreign animal proteins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — horse serum used in tetanus treatment is the example, along with animal hormones or drugs. Complexes lodge in heart, kidneys, skin and joints. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is a physician-supervised challenge used for?","choices":["Confirming transfusion compatibility","Food allergies when other tests are inconclusive","Screening every patient for inhaled allergens","Diagnosing contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the fallback when skin and blood testing leave the question open, and supervision is required because the response can be severe. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does the cross-match test compare?","choices":["Recipient serum against donor serum","Donor red cells against recipient red cells","Recipient serum against donor red cells","Donor serum against recipient red cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the reverse is not performed: even with whole blood there is not enough antibody in donor serum to reach the density on red cells needed for a reaction. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What does prevention of allergy involve?","choices":["Administering epinephrine prophylactically","Modifying environment and behavior","Taking antihistamines daily regardless of exposure","Injecting small amounts of the allergen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is listed first because removing the trigger is the only approach that does not depend on blunting a normal mechanism. The aim is to avoid the allergen entirely. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is familial about allergy?","choices":["A generalized predisposition","The specific allergen a person reacts to","The route by which allergens enter","Nothing — allergy has no familial component"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — families inherit the tendency rather than the particular allergen, which is why relatives may react to entirely different things. The specific allergy is not inherited. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why must ABO antigens be matched in transfusion?","choices":["Transfused cells are permanent replacements","There are no MHC molecules on red blood cells","MHC matching is more important than ABO for red cells","Red cells carry the full set of HLA antigens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so ABO is the critical matching system. You carry antibodies in your serum against the antigens you lack, and mismatch can be fatal through complement activation and lysis. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why does helminth infection not produce allergic disease despite raising IgE?","choices":["Eosinophil numbers fall during infection","Nonspecific IgE competes for Fc receptors","Helminths destroy mast cells directly","IgE is not involved in allergic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, crowding out parasite-specific and allergen-specific IgE. T cell responses are also suppressed in chronic parasitic infection by regulatory T cells. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is required for a type III reaction to occur?","choices":["Prior sensitization with IgE","A T cell mediated memory response","Only a trace of antigen","A large amount of antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is part of why serum sickness follows exposure to foreign animal proteins in quantity rather than to a trace allergen. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the chain of events leading to death in systemic anaphylaxis?","choices":["Fluid leaves the blood and pressure drops","Red cells lyse and block the glomeruli","Immune complexes accumulate in the heart and kidneys","T cells gradually destroy the airway epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, ending in organ damage and asphyxiation from the constricted airways. It causes 500 to 1,000 deaths a year in the United States. Tissue swells and the airways constrict. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What do eosinophils release in an IgE response?","choices":["Complement components","Toxic mediators","Histamine from preformed granules only","Antibodies of the IgE class"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and their defensive role explains the allergic one: they are used against EUKARYOTIC PARASITES, and allergens resemble parasitic antigens. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How many IgE molecules can each mast cell bind?","choices":["Between 10,000 and 40,000","Between 10 and 40","Exactly one","Between 100 and 400"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which explains the scale of the response — a single cell carries an enormous number of trigger points for crosslinking. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the late phase reaction achieve?","choices":["It attracts more cells and sensitizes the tissue","It terminates the allergic response","It produces immediate mast cell degranulation","It clears immune complexes from the tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — eosinophils are drawn in, their cytokines enhance inflammation, and the tissue becomes more sensitive to subsequent allergic reactions. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are the systemic targets of a type I reaction?","choices":["Smooth muscle, mucous glands, nerve","Skin, conjunctiva and nasal mucosa","Red blood cells and platelets","Basement membranes of epithelial tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the responses are vascular dilation and constriction, producing changes in blood pressure and respiration. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which allergens and routes are named for anaphylaxis?","choices":["Inhaled pollens only","Contact with metals and cosmetics","Transfused red blood cells","Bee stings and antibiotics by injection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the variability of both allergen and route is the point — what matters is that the allergen reaches the bloodstream. Serum too, and peanuts by the oral route. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What antibody class mediates type I hypersensitivity?","choices":["IgG and IgM binding cell surface antigens","IgG and IgM binding soluble antigens","No antibody — it is T cell mediated","IgE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the reaction is IMMEDIATE — cell-bound antibody is crosslinked and inflammatory mediators are released from mast cells, basophils and eosinophils. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What happens in the nose during allergic rhinitis?","choices":["Histamine raises capillary permeability","Immune complexes deposit in the nasal basement membrane","Cytotoxic T cells destroy nasal epithelium","Complement lyses the nasal mucosal cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and eosinophils are then attracted from the blood, release inflammatory mediators, and are shed into the nasal passage. The nasal epithelium is activated to make mucus. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the most common form of allergen specific immunotherapy?","choices":["Inhaled allergen challenge","Subcutaneous immunotherapy","Sublingual immunotherapy","Intravenous allergen infusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — small amounts of allergen introduced by injection, improving reactions in about 80 percent of those who complete the process. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What has a four-year trial shown about sublingual immunotherapy for peanut allergy?","choices":["It works only for peanut and no other food","It is effective and safe","It cures peanut allergy permanently in all patients","It requires a whole peanut kernel per dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it appears to provide safety against accidental exposures. It has also been shown effective against kiwi, hazelnut, milk and peach allergies. The dose is about one seventy-fifth of a kernel. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"When is Rhogam administered?","choices":["Only at the time of delivery","Only before the first pregnancy","Only if the first child was affected","At 26 to 28 weeks, and after birth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and also after prenatal invasive tests, abdominal injury or accidental exposure — any event that could mix fetal and maternal blood. The postnatal dose is within 72 hours. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What mediates type IV hypersensitivity?","choices":["IgE bound to mast cells","IgG and IgM with complement","Complement alone, without cells","T cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cytotoxic cells destroy tissue, and because the response requires cellular recruitment rather than preformed antibody, it is DELAYED. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"When does the late phase reaction occur?","choices":["About six hours afterwards","Within the first minute","After several weeks","Only after a second sensitizing dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, mediated by synthesized products such as leukotrienes rather than by preformed granule contents. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which are named as infectious allergy examples of type IV?","choices":["Transfusion reactions and hemolytic disease of the newborn","Serum sickness and the Arthus reaction","Tuberculosis, leprosy, syphilis and histoplasmosis","Hay fever and anaphylaxis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with toxoplasmosis and candidiasis completing the list — chronic infections where the T cell response itself damages tissue. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
